# WHAT A DEPLOYMENT UPLOADS — an allowlist, not a denylist.
#
# Everything a deployment uploads that is not a function is served publicly as
# a static file. The Vercel CLI does NOT read .gitignore once this file exists,
# so a denylist here has to name every local-only thing by hand — and it used
# to miss .pglite/, the local development database, which holds real account
# emails and password hashes. A manual `npx vercel --prod` uploaded it and
# served it at /.pglite/... to anyone who asked.
#
# So: ignore everything at the root, then let back in exactly what the site is.
# A new local file (a database, a log, a dump, notes, a secret) is private by
# default. A new file the SITE needs has to be added below, and forgetting to
# is loud rather than dangerous: dev-server.mjs refuses to serve anything this
# file excludes, and npm run check fails if a page references one.
#
# This applies to Git deployments as well as the CLI.
/*

# the pages, their scripts, the stylesheet and the images they use
!/*.html
!/*.js
!/*.css
!/*.jpg
!/*.jpeg
!/*.png
!/*.webp
!/*.svg
!/*.ico

# the one serverless function and its code
!/api

# what the function build needs
!/package.json
!/package-lock.json
!/vercel.json

# Kept out by the rule at the top, named so nobody "fixes" the allowlist by
# adding them: .env and .env.* (secrets; .env.example too — it documents the
# secrets), .pglite/ (the local database), .git/, .github/, .claude/,
# .vercel/, node_modules/, scripts/, docs/, dev-server.mjs, README.md, the
# source .docx and the working notes, and any *.log or *.stackdump.
